SECTION 34 — SHANNON ENTROPY OF VELOCITY & ANISOTROPY DISTRIBUTIONS
===============================================================================

This section was developed using a scaffold generated with assistance from Claude,
then expanded and completed with additional implementation detail and theoretical
refinement.

The work extends the existing diagnostic framework from Sections 31–33
(Lyapunov exponents, spectral complexity, and phase-space mixing) into a
distribution-level, information-theoretic description of velocity space.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEY COMPONENTS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Differential entropy estimators
  - KDE-based Monte Carlo entropy estimator for low-N shells
  - Histogram-based entropy estimator with proper density normalization
  - Gaussian reference entropy and excess entropy relative to Maxwellian

• Information-theoretic measures
  - KL divergence from a fixed Maxwellian baseline (initial conditions)
  - Jensen–Shannon divergence for time-resolved distribution evolution

• Velocity decomposition
  - Radial velocity v_r
  - Tangential speed v_t
  - Azimuthal velocity v_φ

• Radial shell diagnostics
  - H_r(r,t), H_t(r,t), H_φ(r,t)
  - Entropy anisotropy: ΔH = H_r − H_t
  - Excess entropy relative to Gaussian expectation

• Structure detection
  - KDE peak finding via scipy.signal.find_peaks
  - Identification of multi-modal velocity distributions
    (associated with tidal streams and merger substructure)

• Global + group-level statistics
  - Global entropy evolution over time
  - Group-resolved entropy (inner, mid, outer halo, M31 debris)

• Visualization framework
  - 9 planned figures including:
    • entropy heatmaps
    • KL profiles
    • JS divergence evolution
    • multimodality fraction
    • global entropy time series
    • master summary panel

• Animation design
  - Time-evolving velocity PDFs
  - Entropy anisotropy bar evolution across shells
  - Synchronized diagnostic time-series display

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONCEPTUAL OUTCOME
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section shifts analysis from particle-level chaos diagnostics to
distribution-level information theory.

It enables detection of:
  - multi-stream tidal structure not visible in σ or β
  - non-Gaussian velocity features during mergers
  - phase-mixing processes invisible to second-moment statistics

